  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on comparing variances and favoring higher variance. The individual does not consider or mention any concept related to variance, dispersion, or risk in a statistical sense. Instead, their reasoning is anchored in real-world analogies (lottery tickets), assumptions about entry costs, and the appeal of a large prize when there is no cost. While both Lottery B and the decision reason favor the same lottery (B), the justification is entirely different: the individual’s reasoning is about perceived cost and opportunity, not statistical variability. There is no evidence that variance was part of the deliberation. The information considered (entry cost, real-world lotteries) does not align with the variance-based information, and the justification (no cost → better deal) does not match the rule of preferring higher variance. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used variance as a decision reason.",
